  "description": "MALR (Modality-Aware Label Refinement) is the first method to provide multi-category modality-aware error typing for multimodal PRM training labels. While recent work has demonstrated the value of perception-aware supervision—Perceval provides binary hallucination detection, VL-PRMs structure data into perception/reasoning stages, and PAPO/VPPO/AT-RL incorporate perception signals at RL time—no existing method provides the full error type decomposition at the PRM training data level. MALR fills this gap with a 5-category error taxonomy {perception, symbolic_formulation, calculation, logical_reasoning, cascade} applied to multimodal PRM training data via an ensemble VLM annotation pipeline with confidence-calibrated labeling and human-in-the-loop verification for low-confidence samples. The key empirical argument is that multimodal settings have fundamentally higher error diversity than text-only settings: perception errors account for 67-87% of all VLM errors and are mechanistically distinct from reasoning errors (arising from visual processing, not logical deduction). Binary labels in multimodal thus conflate a dominant, modality-specific error type with minority reasoning errors, creating a much larger information loss than in text-only settings where PathFinder-PRM's {math, consistency} types often co-occur. Furthermore, perception errors cause unique cascade patterns where downstream reasoning appears locally correct but fails due to upstream misperception—a phenomenon that binary labels fundamentally cannot capture. MALR produces typed labels that serve as reusable infrastructure for the entire multimodal PRM field: typed VisualPRM400K dataset, the MALR annotation pipeline as an open tool, and VisualProcessBench-Typed as a typed evaluation benchmark.",
