      "concern": "Why should multimodal error typing outperform text-only error typing (PathFinder-PRM's modest +2.2/+1.5 gains)? Without a strong argument, MALR might achieve similarly modest results.",
      "response": "We provide a detailed information-theoretic argument for why multimodal typed labels should yield substantially larger gains than text-only typed labels. Three factors drive this prediction: (1) ERROR DIVERSITY: In text-only math, PathFinder-PRM's {math, consistency} types have low diversity and frequently co-occur—many errors are simultaneously mathematical and logically inconsistent, so typing provides limited additional information over binary labels. In multimodal, the 5 error types are far more separable: perception errors (misreading a diagram) are mechanistically distinct from calculation errors (wrong arithmetic), which are distinct from cascade errors (correct reasoning on wrong inputs). The information gain from typing is proportional to the separability of the error categories. (2) DOMINANT MODALITY-SPECIFIC TYPE: Perception errors account for 67-87% of all multimodal errors and arise from a completely different computational mechanism (visual encoder processing) than reasoning errors (language model deduction). Binary labels in multimodal thus conflate the dominant error mode with a minority mode that has different causes, different signatures, and different remediation strategies. This is a much larger information loss than in text-only where the two types are more balanced and more correlated. (3) CASCADE PATTERNS: Perception errors cause unique cascade patterns where downstream steps appear locally correct but are wrong due to upstream misperception. This propagation pattern exists only in multimodal settings (text-only rarely has an analogous upstream modality failure that corrupts downstream reasoning). Binary labels cannot capture this at all—every step is independently labeled correct/incorrect without tracking information flow. Typed labels with cascade detection recover this information completely. Quantitative prediction: PathFinder-PRM's +2.2 gain comes from 2 partially-overlapping types in a low-diversity setting. MALR's 5 